                                                         <u> SB 2 X1
                                                         </u> Page  1

Date of Hearing:   March 17, 1999

              ASSEMBLY COMMITTEE ON APPROPRIATIONS 
                    Carole Migden, Chairwoman

        SB 2 X1 (O'Connell) - As Amended: March 15, 1999 

Policy Committee:                               
EducationVote:19-0

Urgency:     Yes                  State Mandated Local  
Program:YesReimbursable:          Yes

 <u> SUMMARY </u> :

This bill requires the State Superintendent of Public  
Instruction (SSPI), with approval from the State Board of  
Education (SBE), to develop a high school exit examination in  
language arts and mathematics.  Specifically, this bill:

1)Requires the SSPI to develop a high school exit exam aligned  
  to the statewide content standards for language arts and math.  
   The SBE is required to adopt a high school exit exam by Oct.  
  1, 2000.

2)Requires all students, beginning with the 2003-2004 class, to  
  pass the high school exit exam to qualify for graduation.

3)Allows students to take the exam beginning in grade 10.

4)Requires the SSPI to contract out for a multiyear evaluation  
  of the high school exit exam.  The preliminary report shall be  
  made to the governor, Legislature, the SSPI, and the SBE by  
  July 1, 2000, with subsequent reports every two years.

5)Requires school districts to notify parents regarding the high  
  school exit exam.


 <u> FISCAL EFFECT </u> :

1)The bill appropriates $2 million in federal funds (Goals 2000)  
  for the development of the high school exit exam.  These funds  
  are provided in the proposed 1999 Budget Act.









                                                         <u> SB 2 X1
                                                         </u> Page  2

2)GF (non-Prop 98) costs of $500,000 annually to contract out  
  for a multi-year evaluation.

3)GF (non-Prop 98) cost of $300,000 annually to the SSPI to  
  administer the development of the test and the contract for an  
  independent evaluation.

4)GF (Prop 98) costs of $3-5 million annually to school  
  districts with high schools to offer the test to high school  
  students in the class of 2003-04, beginning in 2001-02. 

5)GF (Prop 98) costs of $350,000 annually to school districts  
  for a parental notification, beginning in 2001-02.   The bill  
  states 2003-04, but should state 2001-02.

 <u> BACKGROUND </u> :

 <u> 1)STAR </u> (Standardized Testing and Reporting)  (SB 376 /1997).   
  All students in grades 2 through 11 are tested in the Spring  
  of each year on a standardized test of basic skills designated  
  by the State Board of Education.  This test is known also as  
  the Stanford Achievement Test, form 9. This test provides  
  individual student scores that are cross-referenced to the  
  publisher's national test taking population ("national norm").

 <u> 2)Local Proficiency Tests </u>  (AB 65 /1977).  All school districts  
  maintaining grades 6 through 12 are required to establish  
  basic skills proficiency standards and administer assessments  
  that all students must pass in order to graduate.  Tests and  
  standards are locally developed and aligned to local  
  curriculum, provided they address reading comprehension,  
  writing and computational skills.  Different standards and  
  testing procedures are authorized for special education pupils  
  and other pupils with a diagnosed learning disability.   
  Assessment of pupil proficiency is required at least once  
  during grades 4 through 6, once during grades 7 through 9, and  
  twice during grades 10 and 11.  Pupils of limited English  
  proficiency (LEP) are not tested until they have received at  
  least 24 months of instruction, including six months  
  instruction in English. 
 <u> 
3)Current Graduation Requirements </u> .   Current law requires  
  California students to complete state and locally specified  
  courses in order to graduate from high school.  Although  
  attainment of local proficiency standards (described above) is  








                                                         <u> SB 2 X1
                                                         </u> Page  3

  required, there is currently no single statewide test required  
  for high school graduation. 
 <u> 
4)Academic Standards </u> .  Current law calls for the State Board of  
  Education to adopt content and performance standards, but does  
  not mandate the adoption of those standards locally.  To date,  
  the Board has adopted content standards for subjects and grade  
  levels specified in statute.  None of the current tests are  
  "aligned" to state standards.  The SBE plans to augment the  
  STAR test with standards-based questions when it is  
  administered in spring 1999.

<u> 5)Proposed </u> Technical Amendments. The parent notification should  
  commenced in the year 2001-02 for the class of 2003-04.   On  
  page 7, line 28, the billl should be amended to delete  
  "2003-04", and replace with "2001-02". 




